  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies are partly to mostly cloudy this\nafternoon. The weak system that brought the flurries to portions\nof northern Nevada will be shifting off to the east this\nafternoon. With the prospect of clearing this evening, have\ncontinued the trend of keeping patching freezing fog in the\nvalleys across northern Nevada. Overnight lows will be in the 20s.\n\nHigh pressure will slowly build northward across Nevada Tuesday \nthrough Friday. This will result in a warming trend, though the \nridge axis is expected to be dampened by jet energy to the north\nby the THursday timeframe. Highs will be in the 40s and 50s while\novernight lows will be in the 20s. With respect to Thursday, weak\nisentropic lift and limited moisture may bring isolated rain/snow\nshowers to locales north of I-80 by Thursday and Friday. Little \nprecipitation is expected at this time. By Friday afternoon, high \ntemperatures throughout Northern and Central Nevada will be around\nten degrees above normal for this time of year. \n\nHigh temperatures will remain well above seasonal values\nSaturday afternoon. Precipitation ahead of the next storm system\nmay overspread Northern Nevada Sunday. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: NBM forecast was changed during\nthe Thursday and Friday timeframe to add in PoPs and Wx due to\npossible light precipitation occurring at that time. Confidence \nis high with regards to the upper ridge building over northern and\ncentral Nevada. Confidence is moderate in the warming trend that \nwill be taking place this week. Low confidence has been assigned \nto the isentropic lift for Thursday and Friday.",
